    answer: "It depends on the project type, the occupancy, and the authority having jurisdiction — but for most commercial buildings, multi-family housing, and structural work, the answer in Rhode Island is yes. The Rhode Island State Building Code applies statewide, and municipal building officials enforce the stamp requirement at plan review. Single-family homes built to the prescriptive code are generally exempt, but the exemption ends where commercial occupancy, structural complexity, or the coastline begins — and in Rhode Island, the coastline is never far.\\n\\nThe Rhode Island State Board of Registration for Professional Engineers licenses the engineers who may stamp Rhode Island plans, and only a Rhode Island-licensed PE in responsible charge of the work may seal construction documents. Responsible charge means direct supervision and control over the engineering decisions — the board pursues engineers who seal work they did not direct. Firms offering engineering in Rhode Island must meet the state's firm requirements with a licensed professional in responsible charge. Apex Grid Engineering assigns Rhode Island-licensed engineers as engineers of record on Rhode Island projects, so the stamp on your plans belongs to the professional who controlled the design.\\n\\nRhode Island's permitting quirk is the Coastal Resources Management Council — CRMC. Work in tidal waters, on coastal features, and within the council's jurisdiction needs a CRMC assent in addition to the local building permit, a state-level coastal review that runs on its own track with its own engineering standards for shoreline protection, docks, and coastal construction. Providence runs the state's highest-volume plan review through its Department of Inspection and Standards. Newport's historic districts — the colonial downtown and the mansion-era neighborhoods — layer design review over structural permitting, and the city's 18th- and 19th-century building stock means most renovations start with a structural assessment. The 1938 hurricane still shapes the code's memory here: storm surge, wind-borne debris, and Narragansett Bay flood zones drive the structural design of everything near the water.\\n\\nGray areas cluster around the shore. Single-family homes and agricultural buildings are generally exempt from the stamp requirement. Small commercial projects outside the coastal zone sometimes proceed on unstamped drawings — until a change of occupancy or structural alteration triggers the requirement. Anything within CRMC jurisdiction, anything in a mapped flood zone, and any structural work on Newport's historic building stock should be assumed to need stamped drawings; the building official and the CRMC reviewer both have the final word in their own lanes.",
    directAnswer: "Yes for most commercial, multi-family, and structural work — a Rhode Island-licensed PE in responsible charge must stamp the plans. Single-family homes are generally exempt. Coastal work adds CRMC assent alongside the building permit, and Newport's historic stock plus hurricane-era flood rules shape the structural scope.",
